<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   RunState excludes its Room database from every backup and transfer channel.

   This is a deliberate, temporary policy, not a claim that the data is unimportant.
   A restored copy of runstate.db would arrive on a device as a snapshot of whatever
   was last backed up, and the app has no way to reconcile that with anything: there is
   no server synchronization and no restore handling. Device transfer could also leave
   the same permanent run UUIDs on two phones whose copies can then diverge. Until the
   app can identify and reconcile those copies, restoring either would make its
   local-first record ambiguous.

   The exclusion covers the whole database domain rather than the single runstate.db
   file. SQLite writes -wal and -shm sidecar files beside the database, and backing one
   of those up independently of the main file would restore a torn set that matches no
   moment the database was actually in.

   allowBackup stays true. Preferences and other future app data remain eligible; only
   the database domain is withheld, and only until server sync and safe restore handling
   exist to make a restored run meaningful.

   This file is what Android 11 (API 30) and lower read. Android 12 (API 31) and higher
   read data_extraction_rules.xml instead, so both files must carry the same exclusion.
   minSdk here is 26, so both are genuinely in play.
   See https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/data/autobackup
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="database" path="." />
</full-backup-content>
